<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Copenhagen (1660)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1660">1660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Copenhagen was signed on 27 May 1660 between Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, ending the war that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> had renewed in 1658. Concluded after his death, it returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a> and Trøndelag to Denmark-Norway, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a> remained Swedish as agreed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a>. It fixed a frontier between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and Sweden that has lasted to the present.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> and an alliance of Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. It was a smaller follow-up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a>, which had set the boundaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and Norway. Charles X of Sweden would accept nothing less than Akershus county in exchange for returning Trøndelag and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-denmark">Frederick III of Denmark</a> wanted to return to the terms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bromsebro-1645">Second Treaty of Brömsebro</a> of 1645.</p><p>Both kings were stubborn, and mediating powers had to broker the treaty: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the Swedish side, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> on the Danish side. Frederick argued that Swedish control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> would be a lasting threat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> had agreed with England that no single country should hold both shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a>, to prevent a rise in the Sound toll.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When Charles died in February 1660, Frederick proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> void. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> assured the Swedes that they would secure a treaty on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a>'s terms if <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> gave up its claim to Trøndelag. On 8 March 1660 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michiel-de-ruyter">Michiel de Ruyter</a> was ordered to leave the harbour of Landskrona, where he had blockaded the Swedish fleet, and Frederick yielded.</p><p>Talks began on 24 March. The hardest point was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a>, where the islanders had risen against their Swedish occupiers and killed the commander, Johan Printzensköld, and now feared reprisals. Frederick had promised to protect them and demanded the island back. As the mediators' representatives became involved in intrigues, Hannibal Sehested proposed direct talks between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and in a few days the terms were agreed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed on 27 May 1660 in tents halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and the Swedish camp of Carlstad. Sweden kept the territories east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a>: Ven, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a>. Trøndelag, then including Nordmøre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romsdal">Romsdal</a>, went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, with Sweden receiving several nobles' estates in Scania as indemnity. Sweden was exempted from the Sound Dues, Denmark was to pay 3,500 riksdaler a year for the beacons between Skagen and Falsterbo, and prisoners of war were released. The boundaries it confirmed are almost exactly the same today.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Copenhagen (1660)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
